do $seed$ declare source jsonb; candidate jsonb:=$payload${"code": "R-003", "disposition": "revise", "revision": "2026-10-15.1", "content": {"skill": "reading", "title": "A café changes its coffee routine", "scope": "Foundation reading practice; not a full IELTS paper", "instructions": "Answer every question using only the passage. For True/False/Not Given: True agrees, False contradicts, Not Given is not established. For gaps use ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage.", "passage": "A. This is a fictional case study. At the Willow Café, staff used to prepare a large pot of coffee before the morning rush. They thought this would reduce waiting times. However, some coffee remained unsold and had to be thrown away. The manager wanted to reduce waste without making customers wait longer.\n\nB. For two weeks, the café tested a different routine. Staff made smaller batches and wrote down how many cups they sold each half hour. They also recorded the amount of coffee left at closing time. The trial measured waste and service speed; it did not compare the taste of different coffee beans.\n\nC. The records showed that demand was highest between eight and nine. After ten, fewer customers ordered coffee, so staff could prepare less. At the end of the trial, the café was throwing away less coffee, while average waiting times remained almost unchanged.\n\nD. The manager kept the smaller-batch routine but warned staff against treating the first two weeks as a permanent timetable. School holidays and rainy mornings could change demand. The café therefore continued collecting records instead of assuming that every week would be identical.", "questions": [{"n": 1, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Why did the manager introduce the trial?", "options": ["To change the type of beans", "To reduce waste without increasing delays", "To close the café earlier", "To compare customer incomes"], "key": "B", "evidence": "A: reduce waste without making customers wait longer"}, {"n": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which information did staff record?", "options": ["The number of cups sold each half hour", "The age of each customer", "The source of every coffee bean", "The price of nearby cafés"], "key": "A", "evidence": "B: how many cups they sold each half hour"}, {"n": 3, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "The trial compared the taste of different beans.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "False", "evidence": "B: it did not compare the taste of different coffee beans"}, {"n": 4, "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Demand was highest between eight and _____.", "key": ["nine"], "evidence": "C: demand was highest between eight and nine"}, {"n": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Why did staff continue collecting records?", "options": ["They had lost the first records", "The trial had increased waiting times", "Demand could change with circumstances", "Customers refused smaller cups"], "key": "C", "evidence": "D: School holidays and rainy mornings could change demand"}, {"n": 6, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which title best expresses the main idea?", "options": ["How coffee reached Europe", "Why all cafés need identical timetables", "A competition between coffee growers", "Using local records to improve a routine"], "key": "D", "evidence": "A–D describe a local trial, records and adjustment"}], "authorship": "Brains Heist AI-assisted revision; human review pending", "sources": []}, "changes": ["Re-authored passage and evidence-bound questions", "Balanced MCQ positions with plausible distractors", "Contiguous numbering and explicit response limits", "Removed unsupported statistics and explanations"], "blockers": ["Human editorial/key review", "Source and reuse-rights review", "Originality and exposure-family review", "Delivery and scoped pilot checks"], "material_type": "ielts_reading_set", "material_id": "1", "source_snapshot": {"id": 1, "slug": "history-of-coffee", "level": "intermediate", "title": "The History of Coffee", "is_active": true, "questions": [{"id": 1, "body": "Where was coffee originally discovered?", "set_id": 1, "options": ["Ethiopia", "Yemen", "Arabia", "Turkey"], "explanation": "The passage states that coffee is \"a shrub native to Ethiopia.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Ethiopia", "question_order": 1}, {"id": 2, "body": "Who is credited with discovering coffee according to legend?", "set_id": 1, "options": ["Kaldi", "Pope Clement VIII", "A Persian merchant", "An Arabian trader"], "explanation": "The passage mentions \"an Ethiopian goat herder named Kaldi discovered coffee.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Kaldi", "question_order": 2}, {"id": 3, "body": "In which century did coffee reach Europe?", "set_id": 1, "options": ["17th century", "15th century", "16th century", "18th century"], "explanation": "The passage states \"Coffee reached Europe in the 17th century.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "17th century", "question_order": 3}, {"id": 4, "body": "What percentage of world coffee production is Coffea arabica?", "set_id": 1, "options": ["60%", "40%", "70%", "50%"], "explanation": "The passage states \"arabica accounting for approximately 60% of the world's coffee production.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "60%", "question_order": 4}], "created_at": "2025-11-17T12:58:50.309363+00:00", "description": "An academic reading passage about the origins and spread of coffee culture worldwide.", "display_code": "R-003", "est_band_max": 7, "est_band_min": 5.5, "passage_text": "Coffee is one of the most popular beverages in the world. The coffee plant, a shrub native to Ethiopia, was first cultivated for its stimulating properties in the 9th century. According to legend, an Ethiopian goat herder named Kaldi discovered coffee after noticing that his goats became energetic after eating berries from a certain tree.\n\nThe practice of drinking coffee then spread from Ethiopia to the Arabian Peninsula. By the 15th century, coffee was being grown in the Yemeni district of Arabia and by the 16th century, it was known in Persia, Egypt, Syria, and Turkey. Coffee was not only drunk at home but also in public coffee houses called qahveh khaneh. These establishments became important centers for social activity and communication.\n\nCoffee reached Europe in the 17th century, where it initially met with suspicion and controversy. Some religious leaders condemned it as the \"bitter invention of Satan.\" However, Pope Clement VIII tasted the beverage and gave it papal approval. Coffee houses quickly became centers of social activity and intellectual discussion in major European cities.\n\nToday, coffee is a global industry employing millions of people and generating billions of dollars in revenue. The two most commonly grown coffee species are Coffea arabica and Coffea robusta, with arabica accounting for approximately 60% of the world's coffee production.", "required_tier": "free", "duration_minutes": 20, "originality_label": null}, "review_status": "pending_human_review", "human_approval": null, "revision_sha256": "ab8bc11b26d1aba55f1cfe846e496dcfc14602ce75a5a88ddc561ef23b0f410a"}$payload$::jsonb; begin
