do $seed$ declare source jsonb; candidate jsonb:=$payload${"code": "R-004", "disposition": "revise", "revision": "2026-10-15.1", "content": {"skill": "reading", "title": "Coral bleaching: stress and recovery", "scope": "Foundation reading practice; not a full IELTS paper", "instructions": "Answer every question using only the passage. For True/False/Not Given: True agrees, False contradicts, Not Given is not established. For gaps use ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage.", "passage": "A. Reef-building corals are animals. Many live with microscopic algae inside their tissues. The algae use sunlight to make food and supply much of the energy that the corals need. They also contribute to the colours associated with healthy reefs.\n\nB. When conditions become stressful, corals may lose these algae. Increased water temperature is a major cause of this stress, although other factors can contribute. Without the algae, the coral's pale skeleton becomes more visible through its tissue. This change is called bleaching. A bleached coral is not necessarily dead, but it has lost an important source of energy.\n\nC. Recovery is possible if conditions improve and the algae return. Prolonged or repeated stress can make recovery harder and can lead to coral death. It is therefore misleading to describe every bleaching event as either harmless or inevitably fatal. The outcome depends on the conditions and the length of exposure.\n\nD. Researchers monitor water temperature and observe reefs to identify stress and assess recovery. A temperature warning can indicate a risk; it does not prove that every coral at a particular site has died. Direct observations help researchers interpret what is happening locally. This passage gives no numerical forecast for how much reef area will remain in a future year.", "questions": [{"n": 1, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "What do the algae provide for corals?", "options": ["A protective shell made of sand", "Protection from every disease", "A substantial source of energy", "A way to move to deeper water"], "key": "C", "evidence": "A: supply much of the energy that the corals need"}, {"n": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Why does a bleached coral look pale?", "options": ["It grows a new white skin", "It moves into deeper water", "Its skeleton dissolves immediately", "Its skeleton becomes more visible after algae loss"], "key": "D", "evidence": "B: pale skeleton becomes more visible through its tissue"}, {"n": 3, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "All bleached corals are already dead.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "False", "evidence": "B: A bleached coral is not necessarily dead"}, {"n": 4, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "Recovery may occur when conditions improve.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "True", "evidence": "C: Recovery is possible if conditions improve and the algae return"}, {"n": 5, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "One species always recovers faster than every other species.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "Not Given", "evidence": "No comparison of recovery rates between species is supplied"}, {"n": 6, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "What is the role of direct observations?", "options": ["To replace every temperature measurement", "To help interpret local reef conditions", "To prove all bleaching is harmless", "To establish a universal recovery deadline"], "key": "B", "evidence": "D: Direct observations help researchers interpret what is happening locally"}], "authorship": "Brains Heist AI-assisted revision; human review pending", "sources": ["https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/coral_bleach.html", "https://coralreef.noaa.gov/digital-corals/visual-media/infographics/water-temp-coral-bleaching-diagram"]}, "changes": ["Re-authored passage and evidence-bound questions", "Balanced MCQ positions with plausible distractors", "Contiguous numbering and explicit response limits", "Removed unsupported statistics and explanations"], "blockers": ["Human editorial/key review", "Source and reuse-rights review", "Originality and exposure-family review", "Delivery and scoped pilot checks"], "material_type": "ielts_reading_set", "material_id": "2", "source_snapshot": {"id": 2, "slug": "climate-change-coral-reefs", "level": "advanced", "title": "Climate Change and Coral Reefs", "is_active": true, "questions": [{"id": 5, "body": "What are coral reefs often called?", "set_id": 2, "options": ["Rainforests of the sea", "Ocean gardens", "Marine paradises", "Blue forests"], "explanation": "The passage explicitly states coral reefs are \"often called the rainforests of the sea.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Rainforests of the sea", "question_order": 1}, {"id": 6, "body": "What causes coral bleaching?", "set_id": 2, "options": ["Rising ocean temperatures", "Ocean pollution", "Overfishing", "Sea level changes"], "explanation": "The passage explains that \"Rising ocean temperatures cause a phenomenon called coral bleaching.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Rising ocean temperatures", "question_order": 2}, {"id": 7, "body": "What percentage of coral reefs could disappear by 2050 without action?", "set_id": 2, "options": ["Over 90%", "Over 50%", "Over 70%", "Over 30%"], "explanation": "The passage states \"over 90% of coral reefs could disappear by 2050.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Over 90%", "question_order": 3}], "created_at": "2025-11-17T12:58:50.309363+00:00", "description": "An advanced academic passage examining the impact of climate change on coral reef ecosystems.", "display_code": "R-004", "est_band_max": 8, "est_band_min": 6.5, "passage_text": "Coral reefs are among the most biologically diverse ecosystems on Earth, often called the \"rainforests of the sea.\" These underwater structures are formed by colonies of tiny animals called coral polyps, which secrete calcium carbonate to build protective skeletons. Over thousands of years, these skeletons accumulate to create the massive reef structures we see today.\n\nHowever, coral reefs face an existential threat from climate change. Rising ocean temperatures cause a phenomenon called coral bleaching, where corals expel the symbiotic algae living in their tissues, turning white and becoming more susceptible to disease. When water temperatures remain elevated for extended periods, corals can die.\n\nOcean acidification, another consequence of increased atmospheric carbon dioxide, further threatens reef ecosystems. As oceans absorb CO2, their pH decreases, making it harder for corals to build their calcium carbonate skeletons. This process essentially weakens the structural foundation of reef systems.\n\nScientists estimate that without significant action to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, over 90% of coral reefs could disappear by 2050. This would be catastrophic not only for marine biodiversity but also for the millions of people who depend on reefs for food, coastal protection, and income from tourism.", "required_tier": "free", "duration_minutes": 20, "originality_label": null}, "review_status": "pending_human_review", "human_approval": null, "revision_sha256": "e56f301353b1eba70210aa1c487f40fff2ec4c6b19a2312df85a7812c0e98df0"}$payload$::jsonb; begin
