do $seed$ declare source jsonb; candidate jsonb:=$payload${"code": "R-006", "disposition": "revise", "revision": "2026-10-15.1", "content": {"skill": "reading", "title": "How a reserve responds to changing conditions", "scope": "Extended reading practice; fictional case study, not a full IELTS paper", "instructions": "Answer every question using only the passage. For True/False/Not Given: True agrees, False contradicts, Not Given is not established. For gaps use ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage.", "passage": "A. This fictional case study concerns the Fenwater Reserve. Its staff noticed that a shallow pond was drying earlier in summer. The team wanted to understand the change before choosing a response. One dry season alone could not show whether the cause was a lasting shift or ordinary variation from year to year.\n\nB. Staff began recording rainfall, pond depth and the dates when water disappeared from several pools. They used the same measuring points each week so that records could be compared. They also noted whether nearby drainage channels were open. These channels could affect water levels, so the team avoided assuming that rainfall was the only possible explanation.\n\nC. The reserve also monitored plants and insects around the pond. Volunteers counted insects along a fixed route in the morning. They did not compare those counts directly with occasional evening observations because the time of day could influence what was seen. Consistent methods mattered more than collecting a large number of unrelated observations.\n\nD. After reviewing two seasons of records, staff tested a small change to one drainage channel. They kept another section unchanged for comparison. The trial was deliberately limited: changing the whole reserve at once would make it harder to judge the effect. Staff agreed to reverse the change if it damaged nearby habitats.\n\nE. The manager published a short account of the trial, including its limitations. The records could support a local management decision, but they could not establish what would happen in every wetland. The team planned to continue monitoring rather than describe one successful season as a permanent solution.", "questions": [{"n": 1, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Why did staff collect records before acting?", "options": ["To advertise a new visitor centre", "To understand the change before choosing a response", "To prove one dry season was a lasting trend", "To replace every pond in the reserve"], "key": "B", "evidence": "A: wanted to understand the change before choosing a response"}, {"n": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Why were drainage channels recorded?", "options": ["They could also influence water levels", "They measured insect movement", "They showed visitor preferences", "They prevented all rainfall"], "key": "A", "evidence": "B: These channels could affect water levels"}, {"n": 3, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "The reserve received more visitors than any other wetland.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "Not Given", "evidence": "No visitor totals or comparisons with other wetlands appear"}, {"n": 4, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "Some insect species adapted genetically during the trial.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "Not Given", "evidence": "The passage describes counts, not genetic adaptation"}, {"n": 5, "type": "fill_blank", "question": "Volunteers counted insects along a fixed _____.", "key": ["route"], "evidence": "C: along a fixed route"}, {"n": 6, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Why was one section kept unchanged?", "options": ["To attract more visitors", "To increase the number of channels", "To provide a comparison for the trial", "To remove the need for monitoring"], "key": "C", "evidence": "D: kept another section unchanged for comparison"}, {"n": 7, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which paragraph explains the limits of applying local findings elsewhere?", "options": ["Paragraph A", "Paragraph B", "Paragraph C", "Paragraph E"], "key": "D", "evidence": "E: could not establish what would happen in every wetland"}, {"n": 8, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which description best fits the approach of the reserve staff?", "options": ["Certain that all changes are permanent", "Careful and willing to revise decisions", "Uninterested in possible harm", "Dependent on unrelated observations"], "key": "B", "evidence": "D–E: reverse harmful changes, acknowledge limitations and keep monitoring"}], "authorship": "Brains Heist AI-assisted revision; human review pending", "sources": []}, "changes": ["Re-authored passage and evidence-bound questions", "Balanced MCQ positions with plausible distractors", "Contiguous numbering and explicit response limits", "Removed unsupported statistics and explanations"], "blockers": ["Human editorial/key review", "Source and reuse-rights review", "Originality and exposure-family review", "Delivery and scoped pilot checks"], "material_type": "ielts_reading_set", "material_id": "22", "source_snapshot": {"id": 22, "slug": "climate-change-impacts", "level": "Academic", "title": "The Impact of Climate Change on Global Ecosystems", "is_active": true, "questions": [{"id": 26, "body": "According to the passage, what is the primary cause of coral bleaching?", "set_id": 22, "options": ["Increased ocean acidity", "Rising water temperatures", "Pollution from coastal cities", "Overfishing in reef areas"], "explanation": "The passage states that \"elevated sea temperatures cause corals to expel the symbiotic algae living in their tissues, resulting in coral bleaching.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Rising water temperatures", "question_order": 1}, {"id": 27, "body": "The author suggests that polar bears are particularly vulnerable because:", "set_id": 22, "options": ["They cannot adapt to warmer climates", "Their hunting grounds are disappearing", "They are being hunted by humans", "They cannot find mates"], "explanation": "The passage explains that \"as sea ice diminishes, polar bears lose critical hunting platforms they depend on to catch seals.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Their hunting grounds are disappearing", "question_order": 2}, {"id": 28, "body": "The Amazon rainforest has already lost more than half of its original coverage.", "set_id": 22, "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "explanation": "The passage discusses deforestation concerns but does not provide specific percentages of forest loss.", "question_type": "true_false_ng", "correct_answer": "Not Given", "question_order": 3}, {"id": 29, "body": "Scientists predict that some coral species may adapt to warmer temperatures over time.", "set_id": 22, "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "explanation": "The passage mentions that \"researchers have identified heat-resistant coral varieties that may survive in warming oceans.\"", "question_type": "true_false_ng", "correct_answer": "True", "question_order": 4}, {"id": 30, "body": "The migration patterns of many bird species have shifted by an average of _____ kilometers northward.", "set_id": 22, "options": null, "explanation": "The passage states: \"Studies show that migratory birds have shifted their routes an average of 150 kilometers northward.\"", "question_type": "fill_blank", "correct_answer": "150", "question_order": 5}, {"id": 31, "body": "What solution does the author propose for protecting vulnerable ecosystems?", "set_id": 22, "options": ["Complete ban on fossil fuels", "Creating marine protected areas", "Relocating endangered species", "Reducing global temperatures by 5 degrees"], "explanation": "The passage advocates for \"expanding marine protected areas to give ecosystems time to recover and adapt.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Creating marine protected areas", "question_order": 6}, {"id": 32, "body": "Which paragraph discusses the economic impact of ecosystem destruction?", "set_id": 22, "options": ["Paragraph A", "Paragraph B", "Paragraph C", "Paragraph D"], "explanation": "Paragraph C discusses how \"the loss of coral reefs alone could cost the global economy $1 trillion by 2050.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Paragraph C", "question_order": 7}, {"id": 33, "body": "The tone of the passage can best be described as:", "set_id": 22, "options": ["Optimistic and hopeful", "Alarmed but informative", "Pessimistic and fatalistic", "Neutral and detached"], "explanation": "While presenting concerning data, the author maintains an educational approach and offers potential solutions.", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Alarmed but informative", "question_order": 8}], "created_at": "2025-12-03T06:12:19.711253+00:00", "description": "An academic passage discussing how climate change affects various ecosystems around the world, including coral reefs, polar regions, and tropical rainforests.", "display_code": "R-006", "est_band_max": 7.5, "est_band_min": 6, "passage_text": "Climate change is one of the most pressing challenges facing our planet today. The Earth's average temperature has increased by approximately 1.1°C since the pre-industrial era, primarily due to human activities such as burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial processes.\n\nThe effects of climate change are already visible across global ecosystems. Polar ice caps are melting at unprecedented rates, causing sea levels to rise and threatening coastal communities worldwide. Arctic sea ice has declined by about 13% per decade since satellite records began in 1979.\n\nTerrestrial ecosystems are also experiencing significant changes. Many plant and animal species are shifting their ranges toward the poles or to higher elevations in search of suitable habitats. Some species that cannot adapt quickly enough face extinction. Scientists estimate that climate change could threaten up to one million species with extinction in the coming decades.\n\nOcean ecosystems face particular challenges. Rising water temperatures and increased acidity are devastating coral reefs, which support approximately 25% of all marine species. Additionally, changes in ocean currents and temperatures are affecting fish populations and the communities that depend on them for food and livelihoods.\n\nAddressing climate change requires coordinated global action. The Paris Agreement, signed by nearly 200 countries, aims to limit global warming to well below 2°C above pre-industrial levels. However, current commitments may be insufficient to achieve this goal, and many experts call for more ambitious targets.", "required_tier": "free", "duration_minutes": 20, "originality_label": null}, "review_status": "pending_human_review", "human_approval": null, "revision_sha256": "ff0db3930797ed0cfe96281a33e760bc39d0855bda8495bfe23a81f8ac47dec3"}$payload$::jsonb; begin
