do $seed$ declare source jsonb; candidate jsonb:=$payload${"code": "R-007", "disposition": "revise", "revision": "2026-10-15.1", "content": {"skill": "reading", "title": "Automation at a community library", "scope": "Extended reading practice; fictional case study, not a full IELTS paper", "instructions": "Answer every question using only the passage. For True/False/Not Given: True agrees, False contradicts, Not Given is not established. For gaps use ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage.", "passage": "A. In this fictional case study, a community library introduced an automated system for sending reminders and sorting returned books. Managers expected the system to reduce repetitive work. They did not announce that every existing role would disappear. Instead, they planned to review how staff time was used during a three-month trial.\n\nB. Library assistants previously spent part of each morning checking lists and sending standard reminder messages. During the trial, the system handled most of these messages. Staff still dealt with disputed charges and unusual enquiries because these required explanations specific to each visitor. Automation changed the division of work rather than removing every task from the assistants.\n\nC. The trial created new responsibilities. Staff checked messages for errors, helped visitors use the borrowing machines and reported faults to a technician. The library provided training before asking employees to perform these duties. It also kept a staffed desk for visitors who preferred speaking to a person.\n\nD. Not every result was positive. In the first week, an incorrect setting produced reminders for books that had already been returned. Staff paused the reminder function while the setting was corrected. The incident showed why a time-saving system still needed human oversight. A fast process could spread a mistake as easily as it could reduce routine work.\n\nE. At the end of three months, managers reviewed staff reports and visitor complaints before deciding which functions to retain. They considered the reminder system useful after correction, but they did not treat the trial as proof that automation would benefit every library. The library planned another review before expanding the system to other branches.", "questions": [{"n": 1, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which activity was automated during the trial?", "options": ["Resolving every disputed charge", "Writing all personal explanations", "Sending most standard reminder messages", "Deciding which visitors could enter"], "key": "C", "evidence": "B: the system handled most of these messages"}, {"n": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "Which statement best describes the overall view of automation?", "options": ["It should remove every employee", "It always prevents errors", "It has no practical use", "It can help when training and oversight accompany it"], "key": "D", "evidence": "B–E: useful functions alongside training, correction and oversight"}, {"n": 3, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "All jobs requiring creativity are safe from automation.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "Not Given", "evidence": "No information establishes the safety of all jobs requiring creativity"}, {"n": 4, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "Most workers in other workplaces support automation.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "Not Given", "evidence": "No survey or other evidence about opinions in other workplaces is supplied"}, {"n": 5, "type": "true_false_ng", "question": "The library continued sending reminders while the incorrect setting was being fixed.", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "key": "False", "evidence": "D: Staff paused the reminder function while the setting was corrected"}, {"n": 6, "type": "multiple_choice", "question": "What did the library provide before staff took on new duties?", "options": ["Training", "A permanent closure", "A ban on visitor questions", "A shorter trial"], "key": "A", "evidence": "C: provided training before asking employees to perform these duties"}], "authorship": "Brains Heist AI-assisted revision; human review pending", "sources": []}, "changes": ["Re-authored passage and evidence-bound questions", "Balanced MCQ positions with plausible distractors", "Contiguous numbering and explicit response limits", "Removed unsupported statistics and explanations"], "blockers": ["Human editorial/key review", "Source and reuse-rights review", "Originality and exposure-family review", "Delivery and scoped pilot checks"], "material_type": "ielts_reading_set", "material_id": "23", "source_snapshot": {"id": 23, "slug": "ai-future-work", "level": "Academic", "title": "Artificial Intelligence and the Future of Work", "is_active": true, "questions": [{"id": 34, "body": "According to the passage, which sector is most likely to be affected by AI automation?", "set_id": 23, "options": ["Healthcare", "Manufacturing", "Education", "Agriculture"], "explanation": "The passage indicates that \"manufacturing jobs face the highest risk of automation, with up to 47% of positions potentially replaceable.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Manufacturing", "question_order": 1}, {"id": 35, "body": "The author's attitude toward AI in the workplace is:", "set_id": 23, "options": ["Entirely negative", "Cautiously optimistic", "Completely positive", "Indifferent"], "explanation": "The author acknowledges risks but also highlights opportunities for new job creation.", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Cautiously optimistic", "question_order": 2}, {"id": 36, "body": "All jobs requiring creativity are safe from automation.", "set_id": 23, "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "explanation": "The passage notes that \"even creative fields like graphic design and music composition are seeing AI tools emerge.\"", "question_type": "true_false_ng", "correct_answer": "False", "question_order": 3}, {"id": 37, "body": "The majority of workers support the introduction of AI in their workplaces.", "set_id": 23, "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "explanation": "The passage does not provide survey data on worker opinions about AI.", "question_type": "true_false_ng", "correct_answer": "Not Given", "question_order": 4}, {"id": 39, "body": "What does the author recommend governments do to prepare for AI disruption?", "set_id": 23, "options": ["Ban AI development", "Invest in retraining programs", "Increase minimum wages", "Reduce working hours"], "explanation": "The author advocates for \"robust retraining and upskilling programs to help displaced workers transition.\"", "question_type": "multiple_choice", "correct_answer": "Invest in retraining programs", "question_order": 6}], "created_at": "2025-12-03T06:12:19.711253+00:00", "description": "An exploration of how AI and automation are transforming employment across various industries.", "display_code": "R-007", "est_band_max": 7, "est_band_min": 5.5, "passage_text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly transforming the modern workplace. From automated customer service chatbots to sophisticated data analysis tools, AI technologies are becoming increasingly prevalent across virtually every industry.\n\nProponents of AI argue that these technologies will create significant economic benefits. By automating routine tasks, AI can increase productivity and allow human workers to focus on more creative and strategic activities. A recent study by McKinsey Global Institute suggests that AI could add $13 trillion to the global economy by 2030.\n\nHowever, the rise of AI also raises concerns about job displacement. According to the World Economic Forum, automation may displace 85 million jobs worldwide by 2025. Workers in manufacturing, transportation, and administrative roles are particularly vulnerable to automation.\n\nDespite these concerns, many experts believe that AI will ultimately create more jobs than it destroys. The same World Economic Forum report predicts that 97 million new roles may emerge that are more adapted to the new division of labor between humans, machines, and algorithms. These new positions will likely require different skills, emphasizing creativity, critical thinking, and emotional intelligence.\n\nThe key to navigating this transition lies in education and training. Governments and businesses must invest in reskilling programs to help workers adapt to the changing job market. Lifelong learning will become increasingly important as the pace of technological change continues to accelerate.\n\nUltimately, the impact of AI on the future of work will depend on how society chooses to implement and regulate these technologies. With thoughtful planning and inclusive policies, AI has the potential to create a more productive and equitable economy.", "required_tier": "free", "duration_minutes": 20, "originality_label": null}, "review_status": "pending_human_review", "human_approval": null, "revision_sha256": "64415070b4fb8bed02401ecd6ad3c6881576425d70790706dd5dc9fa8e23a49c"}$payload$::jsonb; begin
