do $seed$ declare source jsonb; candidate jsonb:=$payload${"code": "W-004", "disposition": "revise", "revision": "2026-10-15.1", "content": {"skill": "writing", "title": "Technology in Education", "scope": "Writing practice; example response is not a band guarantee", "test_type": "academic", "task_number": 2, "minimum_words": 250, "suggested_minutes": 40, "prompt": "Some people believe that technology has made learning easier and more accessible, while others think it has created more distractions. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Write at least 250 words.", "model_answer": "Technology can make learning more convenient, but it can also compete for a student's attention. In my view, its value depends less on the device itself than on how a teacher and learner choose to use it.\n\nOne clear advantage is access. A student who does not understand an explanation in class can watch another demonstration, read an alternative example or practise a skill again at home. Digital tools can also give quick feedback on routine exercises. For example, a learner studying spelling can identify a mistake immediately and try the word again while the problem is still fresh. These opportunities are particularly useful when classroom time is limited. However, access to a resource does not automatically mean that a student knows how to learn from it.\n\nThe same device can create distractions. A pupil may open a lesson video and then move to messages or unrelated entertainment. Constant switching between activities can interrupt concentration and leave a task unfinished. There is also a risk of accepting an online answer without thinking about it. If students simply copy an explanation, a completed worksheet may give a misleading impression of their understanding.\n\nI therefore believe that schools should use technology with a clear purpose. A teacher might set a short online practice activity, ask students to explain one answer in their own words and then discuss any difficulties. Notifications can be turned off during focused work, and some tasks can still be completed on paper. This combines useful access with habits that protect attention.\n\nOverall, technology can support learning when it helps students practise, understand and receive feedback. It becomes less useful when the activity encourages passive copying or unnecessary distraction. Good teaching remains essential to making that distinction.", "teacher_notes": "Review the response against the relevant four Writing criteria. The model is one possible response, not the only answer and not a certified band. Check claims, organisation, language and task coverage separately.", "authorship": "Brains Heist AI-assisted revision; human review pending", "model_word_count": 288, "sources": []}, "changes": ["Completed the fragment with an original full response", "Normalized task number, test type, word minimum and timing units", "Model clearly labelled as an example, without a band claim"], "blockers": ["Human editorial/key review", "Source and reuse-rights review", "Originality and exposure-family review", "Delivery and scoped pilot checks"], "material_type": "ielts_writing_task", "material_id": "2", "source_snapshot": {"id": 2, "slug": "technology-education", "title": "Technology in Education", "prompt": "Some people believe that technology has made learning easier and more accessible, while others think it has created more distractions. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Write at least 250 words.", "is_active": true, "task_type": "task2", "created_at": "2025-11-17T16:32:23.268633+00:00", "bands_target": "5.5-7.5", "display_code": "W-004", "required_tier": "prime_prep_user", "sample_answer": "In the modern educational landscape, technology has become increasingly prevalent...", "originality_label": null}, "review_status": "pending_human_review", "human_approval": null, "revision_sha256": "421b676ef09a1a3399e6700e94badd2073daa45f76b275534a2808edf99a5163"}$payload$::jsonb; begin
